# halls — the two side halls east and west of the rotunda, and the groin vaults over them.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away.
#
# 1. THE GROIN VAULT: the hardest surface in this building to sample without a seam.
#
#    Each hall is two square bays of 4.05 vaulted as a true groin vault — one barrel running the
#    length of the hall and one across each bay, of the SAME radius, so both crowns land on 6.525
#    and the groins run corner to centre, straight in plan, as a real groin vault's do. The air is
#    the UNION of the barrels and not their intersection; the intersection is a cloister vault and
#    springs along all four sides, and it took a contact sheet to see the difference.
#
#    What that costs a sampler is the sixteen groin creases (four to a bay, two bays, two halls)
#    and the eight horizontal ridges. A groin is the line where two cylinders of equal radius meet,
#    and the dihedral between them OPENS TOWARDS THE CROWN: at the springing the two webs meet at
#    ninety degrees and by the crown they are all but tangent. A distance field has no trouble with
#    the first and every trouble with the last, because within one voxel of a nearly-tangent crease
#    the nearest surface swaps from one web to the other and the gradient turns over. That is where
#    a mesher puts a notch, a sampler puts a stair and a denoiser puts a smear, and it is four
#    metres of it per bay running diagonally across the voxel grid at no useful angle to anything.
#
#    The union is written `smooth=0.02` on purpose. It is the small round the brief asks of every
#    stone arris, applied to the one arris here that cannot be given a `round=` key — and 0.02 is
#    two thirds of a voxel at the contract's metre 32, so the fillet along a groin is a SUB-VOXEL
#    curved feature and not a comfortable one. DO NOT WIDEN IT to make the groins look cleaner. If
#    a groin comes back notched, that is the sampler, which is the question being asked.
#
# 2. A CLOSED ROOM WHERE EMISSIVES ARE THE ONLY LIGHT.
#
#    Neither hall has a window. The only opening in either is the arch into the rotunda: 2.25 wide
#    and 3.825 tall at the far end of a room 4.05 x 8.10 x 4.725, at the bottom of a reveal 0.45
#    deep, and the rotunda beyond it is itself lit only by an oculus. The opening is about 8 m2 of
#    the room's 160 m2 of surface and it faces the wrong way for anything but bounce. Everything a
#    viewer sees in here past the first bay arrives from a sconce or from nothing.
#
#    fittings.clip owns the sconces and has hung four to a hall, on the side walls at the two bay
#    centres, with a bench under each; nothing in this file depends on them existing, and if they
#    ever go away these rooms go black, which is the correct behaviour for a room with no window.
#    DO NOT PUT A WINDOW IN HERE to make it easier to look at — the whole point is that it is not.
#
# 3. BOUNCE OFF A COLOURED FLOOR ONTO A WHITE VAULT — the contract's own words, and this is the
#    only place in the building it can be asked. The floor is TERRACOTTA (168,98,72, rough=220),
#    32 m2 of it between the two halls, with a LAPIS line (44,64,116) and lapis lozenges. Both are
#    materials NOTHING ELSE IN THE BUILDING USES; I checked every fragment's paint rules before
#    choosing them. The vault over it is plaster at 226,222,212. Warm light from a sconce, one
#    bounce off a saturated warm floor, onto a white curved ceiling, with a dark blue band in the
#    same frame to show whether the bounce is coloured or merely bright. If the vault comes back
#    neutral grey, the indirect path is not carrying colour.
#
# 4. WALKABLE, which is the other half of what the brief asked for. THESE ROOMS WERE MEASURED
#    AGAINST THE 2026-08-16 FLOOR — 2.40 m of head height and 1.20 m of clear width over anything
#    a person walks, 2.10 by 1.00 for a mere opening — AND NOTHING HAD TO MOVE. They were already
#    bigger than it by a wide margin, and a room enlarged for no reason is a room whose proportions
#    were decided by a checklist. Measured at metre 8, every one of them closed at both ends, the
#    first four on `halls_rig` (see its note below) and the fifth on the whole building:
#
#      4.750 m over the middle of a bay        --part halls_rig --gap y@10.50,0     (4.725 built)
#      4.625 m under the transverse rib        --part halls_rig --gap y@9.45,0      (4.578 built)
#      3.750 m across the room at the dado cap --part halls_rig --gap z@10.50,2.68  (3.780 built)
#      2.500 m hard against a side wall        --part halls_rig --gap y@10.50,1.78  (2.430 built)
#      4.000 m through the arch, on its axis   --gap y@4.90,0                       (3.825 built)
#
#    The lowest thing in either room is the impost cornice's own bed at 4.05, which is 2.25 above
#    the floor and stands in a 0.27 m strip against the wall. The middle 3.51 m of the room, which
#    is what anybody walks down, clears 4.725. Raising that cornice would cost a module and buy
#    nothing: 4.05 is 1.80 + 5 M and the vault springs off it.
#
#    There is a continuous 3.69 m wide floor from the rotunda to the far wall with nothing to step
#    over. These rooms are what make the rotunda a place rather than a facade.
#
# 5. A FLOOR THAT IS A PAVEMENT AND A WALL THAT IS NOT A PLANE — added 2026-08-16.
#
#    Two kinds of detail, and each is MATTER rather than paint because at 36 mm and 22 mm they are
#    both bigger than a voxel at the contract's metre 32.
#
#    The floor is LAID IN FLAGS, 0.675 (1.5 M) square in the field and coursed along the marble
#    border and the lapis fillet, with joints 36 mm wide cut 42 mm into it. They go through
#    `void_halls` and not through my own block, because the podium is solid to 1.80 under the whole
#    building and a groove cut only out of my 50 mm of foot would be filled by the podium behind it
#    and would not exist. And the granite threshold under the arch — the one piece of floor in
#    these rooms a foot lands on every single time — is WORN HOLLOW, 36 mm deep and dished across
#    the doorway, which is the shape four hundred years of one doorway makes.
#
#    The plaster is NOT FLAT. `halls_air` is displaced by 0.0225 (M/20) of `grain_stone` before it
#    is cut out of the block, so every wall face and the whole of the groin vault undulate. 0.0225
#    is 0.72 of a voxel at metre 32 and is KEPT; it is 0.36 at metre 16 and 0.18 at metre 8 and
#    `usable_displacement` drops it at both, so this is a feature that only exists at the metre the
#    contract names — which is the right answer and not a compromise: below half a voxel a
#    displacement cannot move a surface, only dither it.
#
#    THE FLOOR PLANE IS CUT BACK OUT OF THE DISPLACEMENT. The displaced air is intersected with a
#    box whose bottom is at 1.80, so the walls and the vault wander and the floor stays dead level.
#    A displaced floor is 22 mm of rise between neighbouring columns, everywhere, and the facility
#    reports its worst rise from the whole building. The panels — the lunettes, the tympanum and
#    the blind panel — are cut separately and stay crisp, which is the comparison: a sharp arris
#    and a soft one in the same wall, in the same light.
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45, and every number below is a multiple of M or of M/2, M/4, M/10.
# Written out so nobody has to work backwards:
#
#    4.95   block west face        11 M          1.75   block foot, 0.05 into the podium
#    5.40   room west wall face    12 M          1.80   floor           podium_top
#    9.45   the transverse arch    21 M          2.70   dado top        1.80 + 2 M
#   13.50   room east wall face    30 M          3.60   sconce axis     1.80 + 4 M (fittings')
#   13.95   block east face        31 M          4.05   impost foot     1.80 + 5 M
#   +-2.025 room side wall faces    4.5 M        4.50   springing       1.80 + 6 M
#   +-2.925 block side faces        6.5 M        4.50   arch impost     the same line
#    7.425  west bay centre        16.5 M        5.625  arch crown      4.50 + 2.5 M
#   11.475  east bay centre        25.5 M        6.525  vault crown     4.50 + 4.5 M
#                                                6.975  block top       6.525 + M
#
#   2.025   the vault radius, 4.5 M, and it is HALF THE HALL'S WIDTH and HALF A BAY'S LENGTH at
#           once. That equality is the whole design: it is what makes the bay square, what makes
#           both barrels the same radius, what puts both crowns on one level, and what makes the
#           groins straight in plan. Change the width or the bay and you lose all four together.
#
# WHY THE HALL STOPS AT 13.95 AND NOT AT THE BRIEF'S 15.10 — RE-ARGUED 2026-08-16 AGAINST
# clips/facility/requests/walls-thickness.md, WHICH ASKS FOR THE 1.15 m STRIP BACK. It does not
# come back yet, and there are three reasons standing between it and this room. Only the first of
# them is the one that request is about.
#
#   1. THE CUTTER IS STILL 1.80. walls-thickness.md says in bold: fix windows.clip and doors.clip
#      first, because "a room moved out to 15.10 while a 1.80 cutter is still in flight is a room
#      with a metre-deep slot cut along its wall — worse than the strip it replaces". Measured on
#      origin/main at metre 8, `--part windows_cuts --gap x@3.60,0`: 28.500 m of clear air between
#      the east and west end-wall cuts, so each cut's inner face stands at x = +-14.25. A 1.00 m
#      cutter would have left 30.00. windows.clip's own `windows_gs_cut` still reads
#      `box -0.675 2.70 -0.18  0.675 4.50 1.80`. The first condition is not met.
#
#   2. A HALL REACHING 15.10 HAS A WINDOW IN ITS EAST END, and that is true at any cutter depth.
#      The end-wall openings stand at z = 0 and +-2.70, each 1.35 wide; this room is 4.05 wide, so
#      the one at z = 0 lands squarely in it. "Neither hall has a window" is load 2 at the top of
#      this file — the building's only interior lit by emissives alone — and one window ends it.
#
#   3. 9.70 IS NOT TWO BAYS OR THREE. The room is 8.10 = 2 x 4.05, and 4.05 is at once the bay, the
#      width and twice the vault's radius; that single equality is what makes both barrels the same
#      radius, both crowns one level and the groins straight in plan. 15.10 - 5.40 = 9.70 divides
#      by neither 4.05 nor anything else on the module, so the groin vault — load 1 — cannot
#      survive the move without the room being re-proportioned as well.
#
# So the hall ends at 13.95, clear of the 1.80 cut by 0.25, and the strip x 13.95 .. 15.10 is left
# empty and lit by those three windows — a 1.15 x 5.85 m room-shaped hole with daylight in it,
# ready for whoever adds the next fragment. When the cutters are 1.00, taking the strip is still a
# decision about what these two rooms are FOR, and not a mechanical one; it belongs to whoever owns
# that decision, and this note is here so they can make it with the numbers in front of them.
#
# WHY BOTH HALLS ARE CLOSED AND NOT ONE OF THEM. The brief asks for one hall lit only by sconces;
# the BRIEF's rule 3 asks for symmetry about x = 0 and does not qualify it. A window on one side
# and not the other is the one thing that would make this building look wrong from the air. Both
# halls closed satisfies the load twice and the symmetry exactly, and it costs nothing: the second
# hall is the control — same room, same sconces, same vault, and any difference between the two in
# a render is the renderer's noise and not the building's.
#
# WHAT I LEFT FOR SOMEBODY ELSE. The strips z 2.925 .. 6.60 north and south of each hall, and the
# strip x 13.95 .. 15.10 beyond its east end, are untouched. So is everything above 6.975. Nothing
# in this file reaches into the rotunda except one arch void, which is punched from x = 4.05 —
# 0.75 inside the contract's inner radius of 4.80 — so it goes through a rotunda wall of any
# thickness up to 1.35 without this file knowing what that wall is.
#
# MEASURED, `--clip-part part_halls` at the contract's metre 32: 8,274,164 voxels, 252.51 m3,
# 943.47 m2 of surface, 528,270 distinct visual records, largest identical group 101, sampled in
# 11.5 s from 58,333,053 field evaluations — 7.0 field evaluations to a solid voxel, against 7.9
# for part_rotunda at metre 16, so the vault and its smooth union are not costing the sampler
# anything out of the ordinary. Nothing in this file is infinitely slack; there is no `around`,
# and the one `rotate` (a floor lozenge) is caught in a box of its own so it keeps a bound.
#
# TWO COMPONENTS, AND THAT IS THE RIGHT ANSWER. They are the two halls. `--clip-part halls_mass`
# — one hall — reports ONE component, all of it joined, and `walkable` reports worst rise 0.00 m
# over 108,284 standable columns: the floor is dead flat from the arch to the far wall. The two
# halls are joined to each other, and to the building, through the rotunda's wall, which is not
# mine to build. Whole clip at metre 8: largest component 1,935,345 of 1,936,226 voxels, and none
# of the 881 that are adrift are in this fragment.
#
# Things I got wrong and had to be told by a picture, all of them written where they happened: the
# vault was an intersection and came out a cloister vault; the impost cornice was half a module and
# vanished; it was then at head height; the lunette panels were one and a half voxels deep and did
# not exist; the floor's blue was the wide band and the room looked like a swimming bath.
#
# Four things that are somebody else's — two flat mouldings elsewhere in the building, the `smooth=`
# key, and a way tools/views.ps1 loses a contact sheet — are in clips/facility/requests/halls.md.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE BLOCK — one solid mass, 4.95 .. 13.95 by 6.975, that the room is cut out of
# ================================================================================================
#
# Walls and vault are one piece of stone with a hole in it, which is what a vaulted room is and
# what makes this part certainly one component per hall. It stands 0.05 into the podium at 1.75
# and is filled over the haunches to a flat 6.975 — the level a floor would be laid on if anybody
# ever builds above these rooms.
#
# The side walls come out 0.90 (D), the same as the outer wall of the block; the west wall 0.45 (M)
# because the rotunda's own wall stands behind it; the east wall 0.45 (M) because the strip beyond
# it is somebody else's room and not solid.

let halls_block = box 4.95 1.75 -2.925   13.95 6.975 2.925 round=0.015

# ================================================================================================
# THE AIR — a box to the springing, and a groin vault over it
# ================================================================================================
#
# `halls_room` is the clear room from the floor to the springing line: 8.10 x 4.05 x 2.25.
#
# Over it, three cylinders. `halls_barrel` runs the length of the hall with its axis on the springing
# line at z = 0, so its section is the semicircle that spans the hall's 4.05 width and rises 2.025
# to the crown. `halls_cross_w` and `halls_cross_e` run ACROSS the hall, one to a bay, with their
# axes on the same springing line at the bay centres, so each spans its bay's 4.05 length and rises
# to the same crown. Every one of them has radius 2.025, and h is the FULL length: 8.10 along the
# hall, 4.05 across it.
#
# THEIR UNION IS THE VAULT AND THEIR INTERSECTION IS NOT. Take the ceiling height above springing
# as a function of position: the union gives max(barrel, cross), which is 2.025 along both crown
# lines, zero at the four corners of each bay, and equal on the diagonals — the groin. The
# intersection gives min, which is zero along all four SIDES of the bay: that is a cloister vault,
# it springs from four lines instead of four points, and it has no groins in it at all. The first
# pass here was the intersection and the contact sheet showed a room with no arches in it.
let halls_room     = box 5.40 1.74 -2.025   13.50 4.50 2.025

let halls_barrel   = cylinder  9.450 4.50 0  r=2.025 h=8.10 axis=x
let halls_cross_w  = cylinder  7.425 4.50 0  r=2.025 h=4.05 axis=z
let halls_cross_e  = cylinder 11.475 4.50 0  r=2.025 h=4.05 axis=z

# Clipped to the room in plan and to the springing below. It reaches down to 4.05 so that it is
# certainly one solid with the box under it rather than two shapes touching on a plane.
let halls_vault_box = box 5.40 4.05 -2.025   13.50 6.60 2.025
# The key goes BEFORE the block on a union, and after it on a one-child operation. `union { a b }
# smooth=0.02` parses as a union and then a statement called `smooth`, and the error it gives is
# "unknown statement 'smooth'" three lines further on. BRIEF.md's grammar shows it the other way.
let halls_webs  = union smooth=0.02 { halls_barrel halls_cross_w halls_cross_e }
let halls_vault = intersection { halls_webs halls_vault_box }

# AND THE WHOLE OF IT IS PUSHED ABOUT BY 0.0225 OF GRAIN, which is what makes it plaster on stone
# rather than a mathematical solid. `grain_stone` is the contract's own 0.35 m fbm, so the wall
# undulates on the scale of a hand's span and not of a fingernail, and the vault's webs breathe
# with it — including along the sixteen groins, where the two surfaces meeting at a nearly tangent
# crease now meet at a nearly tangent crease that is also moving.
#
# THE FLOOR IS TAKEN BACK OUT OF IT. `halls_room` is drawn from 1.74 so that the displacement has
# something to bite on below the floor line, and the whole displaced air is then intersected with a
# box whose floor is at 1.80 — so every face except the floor wobbles. A displaced floor is 22 mm
# of rise between neighbouring columns over 32 m2, and the facility reports its worst rise from the
# whole building; the clipping box's other five faces stand 0.075 or more outside the room and the
# displacement never reaches them, so they cut nothing else.
let halls_air_raw = union { halls_room halls_vault }
let halls_air_wob = displace { halls_air_raw grain_stone } amount=0.0225
let halls_air_box = box 5.30 1.80 -2.10   13.60 6.60 2.10

# --- and the floor is a pavement, which is a void and not a shape --------------------------------
#
# Joints 36 mm wide, cut 42 mm down from 1.800 to 1.758. Eleven across the hall on the field's own
# 0.675 (1.5 M) grid, five along it on the same, and four more on the lines where the terracotta
# field gives way to the lapis fillet and the fillet to the marble border — so the pattern the
# paint draws and the pattern the stone is cut to are the same pattern.
#
# THEY REACH THE WALL AND THEY DO NOT CUT THE DADO. `void_halls` is written as my air LESS MY OWN
# MASS, so a joint that runs under the dado's foot is subtracted from the void by the dado itself
# and stops dead at its face. That is the same property the file already relies on to stop another
# fragment leaving a wall in my room, used the other way round.
let halls_fl_j_x = repeat { box  9.432 1.758 -2.025    9.468 1.815 2.025 } x=0.675 nx=5
let halls_fl_j_z = repeat { box  5.400 1.758 -0.018   13.500 1.815 0.018 } z=0.675 nz=2
let halls_fl_j_b = union {
    box  5.832 1.758 -2.025    5.868 1.815  2.025
    box 13.032 1.758 -2.025   13.068 1.815  2.025
    box  5.400 1.758  1.557   13.500 1.815  1.593
    box  5.400 1.758 -1.593   13.500 1.815 -1.557
}

# The threshold, worn. A dished ellipsoid 36 mm deep and 1.80 across, sunk into the one piece of
# floor in these rooms that every foot lands on, on the axis of the only door. It is 0.56 along the
# way you walk and 1.80 across it, which is the shape a doorway wears rather than the shape a room
# does, and it stops 0.22 short of the reveal at each side so the arris of the opening stays sharp.
let halls_fl_wear = ellipsoid 5.20 1.845 0 rx=0.28 ry=0.081 rz=0.90

let halls_floor_cut = union { halls_fl_j_x halls_fl_j_z halls_fl_j_b halls_fl_wear }

let halls_air = union {
    intersection { halls_air_wob halls_air_box }
    halls_floor_cut
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE ARCH INTO THE ROTUNDA — the only opening in either hall
# ================================================================================================
#
# 2.25 wide (5 M), the impost at 4.50 (6 M above the floor, so the clear height at the jamb is 2.70
# and the brief's 2.40 for a doorway is met at the jamb and not merely on the centre line), a
# semicircular head of 1.125 crowning at 5.625.
#
# It is punched from x = 4.05, which is 0.75 inside the contract's rotunda radius of 4.80, so it
# goes clean through whatever the rotunda builds there and this file never has to know.
#
# IT RUNS TO 5.76, AND IT USED TO RUN TO 5.45. That was reasoned as "0.05 past my own west face,
# so the reveal is cut past its own rounding" -- true of the WALL and false of everything standing
# on it. THE PART below cuts the dado, the impost, the responds and the rib with this void so that
# nothing crosses the opening, and those members do not stop at the wall face: they project into
# the room. Measured on the west wall, where the arch is:
#
#   dado body      to 5.445   cut
#   dado cap       to 5.535   0.085 left standing
#   dado ovolo     to 5.535   0.085 left standing
#   impost bed     to 5.580   0.130 left standing
#   impost corona  to 5.670   0.220 left standing
#
# What that draws is two bars straight across the doorway: the dado at waist height, in porphyry,
# so it reads RED, and the impost above head height in the wall's own stone, so it reads WHITE. It
# looks exactly like somebody barred the room off. Reported from playing, both halls, and it is the
# same 0.220 in each because the fault is a number rather than a shape.
#
# 5.76 is 0.09 past the deepest of them, which is the M/5 this file already uses for a projection
# it wants to be certainly clear of a face. A void that stops at the wall cuts the wall; a void
# that has to cut what STANDS on the wall has to reach past the furthest thing standing there, and
# the number to check it against is the corona, not the face.
let halls_arch_jamb = box 4.05 1.80 -1.125   5.76 4.50 1.125
let halls_arch_head = cylinder 4.905 4.50 0  r=1.125 h=1.71 axis=x
let halls_arch_void = union { halls_arch_jamb halls_arch_head }

# ================================================================================================
# THE SUNK PANELS — one under each wall arch, one in the east tympanum
# ================================================================================================
#
# Over the impost cornice each side wall carries a semicircular wall arch, 4.05 across and 2.025
# high, one to a bay: that is the end of the cross barrel, and the flat wall under it is the
# lunette. A panel is sunk into it 0.09 (M/5) inside a margin of 0.225, which is the same radius
# struck 0.225 smaller and a floor 0.225 above the springing.
#
# 0.09 and not 0.045. The first pass sank them one and a half voxels and they did not exist: in a
# room whose only light is a sconce below them there is no grazing sun to find a shallow arris,
# and a panel that only a raking light can see is a panel nobody will ever see here. The sub-voxel
# work in this fragment is in the dado, where a lamp is level with it.
#
# The east wall carries the end of the long barrel in the same way, and gets the same panel.
let halls_lun_face = box 5.40 4.725 2.025   13.50 6.60 2.115
let halls_lun_w = intersection { cylinder  7.425 4.50 0 r=1.80 h=6 axis=z  halls_lun_face }
let halls_lun_e = intersection { cylinder 11.475 4.50 0 r=1.80 h=6 axis=z  halls_lun_face }
let halls_lunettes = mirror { union { halls_lun_w halls_lun_e } } axis=z

let halls_tymp = intersection {
    cylinder 13.50 4.50 0 r=1.80 h=0.30 axis=x
    box 13.50 4.725 -2.025   13.590 6.60 2.025
}

# An arched panel in the east wall under the cornice, 1.35 across (3 M) and 0.09 (M/5) deep, from
# the dado top at 2.70 to a head springing at 3.375 and crowning at 4.05 — under the impost, so the
# cornice runs unbroken over it. It is the far wall of a room eight metres long and the one thing
# standing at the end of it; requests/halls.md says what could go in front of it.
let halls_blind = intersection {
    union { box 13.50 2.70 -0.675  13.59 3.375 0.675
            cylinder 13.545 3.375 0 r=0.675 h=0.09 axis=x }
    box 13.50 2.70 -0.675   13.59 4.05 0.675
}

let halls_cuts  = union { halls_air halls_arch_void halls_lunettes halls_tymp halls_blind }
let halls_shell = difference { halls_block halls_cuts }

# ================================================================================================
# THE DADO — 1.80 to 2.70, two modules, round all four walls
# ================================================================================================
#
# The body and the cap are FRAMES — the difference of two boxes — so one shape lines all four walls
# and every mitre is free. Only the ovolo between them has to be written as runs, because a
# moulding is a straight length by definition.
#
#   1.750 - 2.565   body    0.045 proud (M/10)
#   2.565 - 2.655   ovolo   swelling from the body's face to 0.135 (0.3 M)
#   2.655 - 2.700   cap     0.135 proud
#
# The run key is not optional and it is not cosmetic: with `run=x` the section is read in (z, y),
# with the default `run=z` it is read in (x, y). A moulding written 8 m long across its own
# projection is a flat slab, and it does not warn.
let halls_dado_body = difference {
    box 5.355 1.75 -2.070   13.545 2.565 2.070
    box 5.445 1.70 -1.980   13.455 2.60  1.980
}
let halls_dado_cap = difference {
    box 5.265 2.655 -2.160   13.635 2.70 2.160
    box 5.535 2.60  -1.890   13.365 2.75 1.890
}
let halls_dado_ov_n = ovolo  5.400 2.565  1.980   13.500 2.655  1.890 run=x
let halls_dado_ov_w = ovolo  5.445 2.565 -2.025    5.535 2.655  2.025
let halls_dado_ov_e = ovolo 13.455 2.565 -2.025   13.365 2.655  2.025
let halls_dado = union {
    halls_dado_body halls_dado_cap
    mirror { halls_dado_ov_n } axis=z
    halls_dado_ov_w halls_dado_ov_e
}

# THE SUNK PANELS IN IT, AND THE ONE SUB-VOXEL FEATURE IN THIS ROOM.
#
# Five panels to a hall — two to each side wall, between the responds, and one on the east wall —
# 0.405 tall (0.9 M) from 2.025 to 2.430, sunk 0.0225 INTO A FACE THAT IS ONLY 0.045 PROUD. That
# is under a voxel deep at the contract's metre 32 and it leaves the same again of dado standing
# behind it: a step of two thirds of a voxel, in the darkest coloured surface in the building,
# lit by a sconce whose flame stands 1.44 m above the middle of it and 0.25 m out from the wall —
# which is to say by a light raking across it at about ten degrees. That is the whole test: a step
# of two thirds of a voxel in shadow
# is invisible, and a sub-voxel step under a raking lamp either draws a line or it does not.
#
# DO NOT DEEPEN THESE. The lunette panels above them are 0.09 for exactly the opposite reason,
# and the pair of them in one room is the comparison.
let halls_dado_pan_n = union {
    box  6.075 2.025 1.980    9.000 2.430 2.0025
    box  9.900 2.025 1.980   12.825 2.430 2.0025
}
let halls_dado_pan_e = box 13.455 2.025 -1.350   13.4775 2.430 1.350
let halls_dado_pans = union { mirror { halls_dado_pan_n } axis=z  halls_dado_pan_e }

# ================================================================================================
# THE IMPOST CORNICE — 4.05 to 4.50, one module, and the line the vault springs from
# ================================================================================================
#
#   4.05 - 4.23   cyma reversa   from the wall face out to 0.18 (0.4 M)
#   4.23 - 4.50   corona         0.27 proud (0.6 M), a plain bold band with a flat soffit
#
# It projects further than anything else in the room, which is the classical order of things: the
# corona is the last member before the vault and the shadow under it is what draws the springing
# line right round a room that has no other horizontal above head height. A whole module tall and
# not half of one, which the first pass tried: at 0.225 the cornice read as two scratches on the
# wall from the middle of the floor, and this is the only string in a room 8 m long.
#
# ITS SOFFIT IS AT 4.05, WHICH IS 2.25 ABOVE THE FLOOR, AND THAT IS WHY THE VAULT SPRINGS AT 4.50
# AND NOT AT 4.05. The first pass sprang the vault at 4.05 and put this cornice under it from 3.60,
# which is 1.80 above the floor — a bold 0.27 projection at exactly the height of somebody's head,
# and 0.30 under the brief's 2.10. Raising the springing by one module fixed it, and cost nothing
# except 0.45 of stone above: the crown went to 6.525 and the block top to 6.975, both still module
# multiples, and the room went from squat to properly proportioned at the same time.
let halls_imp_bed_n = cyma_reversa  5.400 4.050  2.025   13.500 4.230  1.845 run=x
let halls_imp_bed_w = cyma_reversa  5.400 4.050 -2.025    5.580 4.230  2.025
let halls_imp_bed_e = cyma_reversa 13.500 4.050 -2.025   13.320 4.230  2.025
let halls_imp_corona = difference {
    box 5.310 4.23 -2.115   13.590 4.50 2.115
    box 5.670 4.17 -1.755   13.230 4.55 1.755
}
let halls_impost = union {
    mirror { halls_imp_bed_n } axis=z
    halls_imp_bed_w halls_imp_bed_e
    halls_imp_corona
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE RESPONDS — 0.45 wide (M), 0.18 proud (0.4 M), at the ends of each bay
# ================================================================================================
#
# Three to a wall: one at each end of the room and one under the transverse arch at 9.45. They
# stand proud of the dado cap by 0.045 and are capped by the impost cornice, which projects past
# them by the same. Built on +z and folded.
let halls_resp_one = box  5.400 1.75 1.845    5.850 4.50 2.070 round=0.012
let halls_resp_two = box  9.225 1.75 1.845    9.675 4.50 2.070 round=0.012
let halls_resp_thr = box 13.050 1.75 1.845   13.500 4.50 2.070 round=0.012

# THERE WERE SIX SCONCE CORBELS HERE AND THEY ARE GONE ON PURPOSE. This file grew a corbel on each
# respond, at the bay ENDS, for fittings.clip to stand a light on. fittings.clip had already read
# this room and hung its four sconces at the bay CENTRES with benches under them, which is the
# better answer — a light in the middle of a bay lights the bay, and a light on the pier between
# two lights the wall. Six empty corbels beside four lit sconces is a scheme with two ideas in it,
# so the corbels went and the sconces stayed. The wall between the dado cap at 2.70 and the impost
# soffit at 4.05 is left plain, which is what that fragment wants of it.

let halls_responds = mirror {
    union { halls_resp_one halls_resp_two halls_resp_thr }
} axis=z

# ================================================================================================
# THE TRANSVERSE ARCH — a band 0.45 wide standing 0.135 below the vault at 9.45
# ================================================================================================
#
# The one rib in the room, on the line where the two bays meet, springing off the middle respond.
# It is the vault's own cylinder less a cylinder 0.135 smaller, caught in the 0.45 band, and then
# grown 0.012 by `round` so that its two arrises are turned rather than sharp — which is why the
# numbers below are each 0.012 short of the ones the arch actually has.
let halls_rib_core = intersection {
    difference {
        cylinder 9.45 4.50 0 r=2.013 h=0.426 axis=x
        cylinder 9.45 4.50 0 r=1.902 h=0.60  axis=x
    }
    box 9.237 4.50 -2.013   9.663 6.60 2.013
}
let halls_rib = round { halls_rib_core } by=0.012

# ================================================================================================
# THE ARCHIVOLT — the dressing round the arch into the rotunda
# ================================================================================================
#
# A band 0.225 (M/2) wide carried right round jamb and arch and standing 0.18 (0.4 M) proud, which
# is further out than the dado cap, so the dado can run behind it without being cut. Two small
# impost blocks mark where the arch springs.
let halls_arv_out = union {
    box 5.40 1.75 -1.350   5.58 4.50 1.350
    cylinder 5.49 4.50 0 r=1.350 h=0.18 axis=x
}
let halls_arv_in = union {
    box 5.35 1.70 -1.125   5.63 4.50 1.125
    cylinder 5.49 4.50 0 r=1.125 h=0.30 axis=x
}
let halls_arv_band = difference { halls_arv_out halls_arv_in }
let halls_arv_imp  = mirror { box 5.40 4.365 1.125   5.625 4.50 1.485 round=0.010 } axis=z
let halls_archivolt = union { halls_arv_band halls_arv_imp }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================
#
# Everything that stands IN the air is cut by the arch void before it is added, so no cornice and
# no dado crosses the opening. The archivolt is not in that cut: it is the dressing of the opening
# and it stops exactly on the reveal.
let halls_trim = difference {
    union { halls_dado halls_impost halls_responds halls_rib }
    halls_arch_void halls_dado_pans
}

let halls_mass = union { halls_shell halls_trim halls_archivolt }

# --- the measuring rig, which is a ruler and not a room ------------------------------------------
#
# `--gap y@x,z` on the whole facility reports the LONGEST unbroken run of air down a column, and
# over the middle of this floor the longest run of air is not this room: it is the five metres
# between the top of my block at 6.975 and the underside of the roof. BRIEF.md says to use a probe
# whose box is one storey tall and a fragment's box is the whole building's, so the rig is this
# hall with the two surfaces that close it put back — the floor at 1.80 and a lid on the block's
# own top at 6.975 — both run to the ends of the clip so no run outside the storey can win:
#
#     bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility.clip --part halls_rig --metre 8 --gap y@10.50,0
#
# Nothing here is part of the building. It is east-hall only, unmirrored, because a ruler does not
# need two ends.
# EVERY ONE OF THESE RUNS TO THE END OF THE SAMPLED ARRAY. The sampler settles the box to the
# SHAPE and not to the `bounds` line, so a floor drawn to y = -1.00 leaves metres of array under it
# and `longest_clear` finds that instead of the room. A ruler has to be longer than the thing it
# measures in every direction it does not measure.
let halls_rig_floor = box 4.95 -12.00 -16.500   13.95  1.80  8.500
let halls_rig_lid   = box 4.95  6.975  -2.925   13.95 25.00  2.925
let halls_rig_n     = box 4.95 -12.00   2.940   13.95 25.00  8.500
let halls_rig_s     = box 4.95 -12.00 -16.500   13.95 25.00 -2.940
let halls_rig = union { halls_mass halls_rig_floor halls_rig_lid halls_rig_n halls_rig_s }

let part_halls = mirror { halls_mass } axis=x

# What this part removes from the building: the air of the room, and the arch through the rotunda's
# wall, less every stone I put back in either. Written as a difference against my own mass for the
# reason vestibule.clip gives — it takes away anything another fragment leaves standing in my room
# while it cannot touch a stone of mine, and it cannot go stale when a member is added.
let void_halls = mirror { difference { union { halls_air halls_arch_void } halls_mass } } axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Later coats cover earlier ones. Shell first, then the floor built up from its field to its inlay,
# then the stone standing on the floor. `below=0.02` on every rule and never 0: the manifest
# displaces the whole building by 12 mm of grain at the end, and a test at zero misses the voxels
# the displacement made and leaves a rind of the base coat over the part.
#
# Every zone below is one of my own shapes folded to both halls, so the west hall is painted by the
# same rules as the east and cannot drift.

let halls_face = mirror { halls_shell } axis=x
paint plaster where=halls_face below=0.02

# The sunk panels, grown enough to catch the voxels that line them rather than the air in them.
# Stucco rather than plaster: eighteen values warmer and thirty rougher, which is the whole of what
# separates a sunk panel from the field it is sunk in when neither has an edge the sampler can hold.
let halls_panel_face = mirror { round { union { halls_lunettes halls_tymp halls_blind } } by=0.03 } axis=x
paint stucco where=halls_panel_face below=0.02

# AND THE BLIND PANEL IN PORPHYRY, WHICH IS FOR THE STATUE AND NOT FOR ITSELF.
#
# fittings.clip stands a marble figure in front of this panel -- "the far wall of a room eight
# metres long and the one thing standing at the end of it", which is what this file's own note
# above calls it. A white figure against a stucco panel eighteen values from limestone has nothing
# to be seen against, and the entire silhouette -- the head, the shoulders, the arm, the scroll --
# was being spent on a background of the same brightness. Sculpting it further would have bought
# nothing; the ground is what was missing.
#
# Porphyry, because the dado in this room is already porphyry, so the palette is not widened by a
# colour, and because rotunda.clip does exactly this a room away: a verde lining behind a gilt urn,
# for the same reason and with the same argument written under it.
#
# The lunettes and the tympanum keep their stucco. Only the panel with somebody standing in front
# of it changes.
let halls_blind_face = mirror { round { halls_blind } by=0.03 } axis=x
paint porphyry where=halls_blind_face below=0.02

# --- the floor ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Thin slabs from 1.70 to 1.86, straddling the floor plane so they catch its surface and nothing
# under it. Terracotta field, a marble border 0.45 wide (M), a lapis fillet 0.225 (M/2) inside it,
# and five lapis lozenges on the centre line at 1.35 (3 M) apart with a diagonal of 0.90 (D).
#
# TERRACOTTA AND LAPIS ARE USED NOWHERE ELSE IN THIS BUILDING. That is deliberate and it is half of
# what this fragment is for: see load 3 at the top. The wide band is the white one and the blue is
# a line and five lozenges — the first pass had the blue as the 0.45 border and the room came back
# looking like a municipal swimming bath. Lapis at 44,64,116 is the darkest thing in the palette
# and it is a drawn line in a floor, not a field.
let halls_fl_zone_e = box 5.40 1.70 -2.025   13.50 1.86 2.025
let halls_fl_zone   = mirror { halls_fl_zone_e } axis=x
paint terracotta where=halls_fl_zone below=0.02

let halls_fl_field  = box 5.85 1.60 -1.575   13.05 1.96 1.575
let halls_fl_border = mirror { difference { halls_fl_zone_e halls_fl_field } } axis=x
paint marble where=halls_fl_border below=0.02

let halls_fl_inner  = box 6.075 1.60 -1.350   12.825 1.96 1.350
let halls_fl_fillet = mirror { difference { halls_fl_field halls_fl_inner } } axis=x
paint lapis where=halls_fl_fillet below=0.02

let halls_fl_loz_one = intersection {
    rotate { box -0.318 1.70 -0.318   0.318 1.86 0.318 } y=0.125
    box -0.46 1.70 -0.46   0.46 1.86 0.46
}
let halls_fl_loz = mirror {
    translate { repeat { halls_fl_loz_one } x=1.35 nx=2 } 9.45 0 0
} axis=x
paint lapis where=halls_fl_loz below=0.02

# The threshold, in granite, because it is the one piece of floor in these rooms a foot lands on
# every time and the one that has light on it from the rotunda.
let halls_thresh = mirror {
    intersection { halls_mass  box 4.90 1.70 -1.125   5.50 1.86 1.125 }
} axis=x
paint granite where=halls_thresh below=0.02

# --- the stone standing on it -----------------------------------------------------------------------
let halls_stone = mirror { union { halls_impost halls_responds halls_rib halls_archivolt } } axis=x
paint marble where=halls_stone below=0.02

let halls_dado_face = mirror { halls_dado } axis=x
paint porphyry where=halls_dado_face below=0.02
